import{_ as t,o,c as i,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.CzpyQB0j.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Histoire or Storybook as the home of the design system documentation","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/research/histoire-for-the-design-system-docs.md","filePath":"docs/research/histoire-for-the-design-system-docs.md"}'),r={name:"docs/research/histoire-for-the-design-system-docs.md"};function a(d,e,n,c,h,l){return o(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="histoire-or-storybook-as-the-home-of-the-design-system-documentation" tabindex="-1">Histoire or Storybook as the home of the design system documentation <a class="header-anchor" href="#histoire-or-storybook-as-the-home-of-the-design-system-documentation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Histoire or Storybook as the home of the design system documentation&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Research notes, 14 November 2026. Primary sources only: the projects&#39; own documentation, source files, changelogs and the npm registry. Where I could not confirm something it says so. Nothing here has been run in this repo.</p><h2 id="short-answer" tabindex="-1">Short answer <a class="header-anchor" href="#short-answer" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Short answer&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Histoire could technically hold most of it, but it is the weaker choice today, and the owner&#39;s goal (one place, with checks that fail CI) is already met by Storybook. Keep Storybook (<a href="./../adr/0028-storybook-is-the-component-workshop-of-the-design-system">ADR 0028</a>) and grow it into the whole design system home with MDX pages.</p><ul><li>Histoire&#39;s last published version is <code>1.0.0-beta.1</code>, from 7 January 2026, and its <code>latest</code> tag is still a beta. Vite 8 support, the browser test runner and the accessibility plugin exist only on its <code>main</code> branch, not in a published release (checked on npm on 14 November 2026).</li><li>Histoire can make standalone Markdown pages, but they are static Markdown. Live component samples, swatches and iframes would have to be hand-built Vue stories. Storybook MDX mixes prose, live stories and ready-made colour and type blocks in one page.</li><li>Both build to static files and both can sit under a sub-path. Storybook is already doing it on the docs site.</li></ul><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Context&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>web/ui</code> is Vue 3.5, Tailwind 4.3.5 and DaisyUI 5.7.50, pinned exactly (<a href="./../adr/0023-the-websites-share-one-vue-and-daisyui-design-system">ADR 0023</a>). <code>web/storybook</code> pins Storybook 10.6.7, <code>@storybook/vue3-vite</code>, <code>@storybook/addon-a11y</code>, <code>@storybook/addon-docs</code>, <code>@storybook/addon-vitest</code> and Vitest 5.1.0 (<code>web/storybook/package.json</code>).</li><li>The docs site is VitePress. Its top menu is <code>themeConfig.nav</code> in <code>site/.vitepress/config.mts</code>, and <code>site/copy-docs.mjs</code> builds Storybook and copies it to <code>public/storybook/</code>.</li><li>The Design system section today holds written rules, token swatches, live component samples with classes, Android and iOS component descriptions, and plain HTML mockups in iframes (<code>site/.vitepress/config.mts</code>, sidebar key &quot;Design system&quot;).</li></ul><h2 id="_1-version-cadence-maintenance-compatibility" tabindex="-1">1. Version, cadence, maintenance, compatibility <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-version-cadence-maintenance-compatibility" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Version, cadence, maintenance, compatibility&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Histoire</th><th>Storybook</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Latest on npm</td><td><code>1.0.0-beta.1</code>, 7 January 2026</td><td><code>10.6.7</code>, 10 November 2026</td></tr><tr><td>Recent releases</td><td>alpha.1 (22 Jan 2025), alpha.2 (21 Feb), alpha.3 (30 Jul), alpha.4 (20 Oct), alpha.5 (20 Nov), beta.1 (7 Jan 2026), nothing since</td><td>10.5.9 (18 Aug 2026), 10.6.0 (2 Sep), 10.6.1 (29 Sep), 10.6.2 (12 Oct), 10.6.3 (20 Oct), 10.6.4 (26 Oct), 10.6.5 (27 Oct), 10.6.6 (5 Nov), 10.6.7 (10 Nov)</td></tr><tr><td>Vite</td><td>Published beta.1 peer range is <code>^7.3.0</code>. <code>main</code> declares <code>^7.0.0 || ^8.0.0</code></td><td><code>@storybook/vue3-vite</code> 10.6.7 peers <code>^5 || ^6 || ^7 || ^8</code></td></tr><tr><td>Vue</td><td><code>@histoire/plugin-vue</code> beta.1 peers <code>vue ^3.5.26</code>. The changelog says beta.1 fixes story collection on Vue 3.5.26 and later</td><td>Vue 3 through <code>@storybook/vue3-vite</code></td></tr><tr><td>Tailwind 4</td><td>No statement of support found. The changelog lists a &quot;Tailwind design system story styling&quot; fix in alpha.5, and <code>main</code> has a <code>test:tailwind:integration</code> script</td><td>Already works in this repo through <code>@tailwindcss/vite</code> in <code>viteFinal</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>What this means:</p><ul><li>The 0.17 line stopped on 9 April 2024, then 1.0 went through five alphas and one beta over about a year. There has been no published release for about ten months. I could not read the repo&#39;s commit dates or issue counts (GitHub API access was refused), so &quot;maintenance status&quot; is judged from the npm record only. The <code>main</code> branch has clearly moved on (new docs for visual testing, embedding, an MCP server, Vite 8), so the project is not abandoned, but its newest work is unreleased.</li><li>The Histoire docs site describes <code>main</code>. Pages marked &quot;1.0-beta.2+&quot; describe unreleased features, and the docs say to try them with the project&#39;s continuous releases.</li><li>Histoire requires Node 22.12 or later and says &quot;Vite 7 or 8&quot; on its getting-started page, where Vite 8 is marked as &quot;1.0-beta.2+&quot;. The repo has Vite 8 in play (Storybook&#39;s Vite peer range allows it), so a published Histoire today would pin the repo to Vite 7 (inference from the peer range).</li></ul><h2 id="_2-standalone-written-pages-and-how-rich-they-can-be" tabindex="-1">2. Standalone written pages and how rich they can be <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-standalone-written-pages-and-how-rich-they-can-be" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Standalone written pages and how rich they can be&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Histoire:</p><ul><li>A file ending <code>.story.md</code> with no sibling story file becomes a virtual story that renders the Markdown as a page. Front matter sets <code>id</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>icon</code>, <code>iconColor</code> and <code>group</code>. Pages can link to stories and to each other by relative path.</li><li>Markdown also attaches to a story as a <code>&lt;docs lang=&quot;md&quot;&gt;</code> block or a sibling <code>.md</code> file.</li><li>The renderer is markdown-it with <code>html: true</code>, anchors, attributes and emoji, and the config has a <code>markdown</code> hook to extend it. Raw HTML in a page is therefore passed through. I did not test whether an <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> inside such a page renders in the workbench, and there is no documented way to put a Vue component inside Markdown.</li><li>Live samples, swatches and the plain-HTML mockup iframes would be written as <code>.story.vue</code> files, with variants, not as prose pages. There is no ready-made colour or type block. The <code>tree</code> config can group and order pages, and the docs say stories can be placed by title path, so a &quot;Design system&quot; tree is possible.</li><li>Tokens could be shown as a Vue story that reads <code>tokens.css</code> or <code>design/tokens.json</code>. This is the same work as a Storybook MDX page but without a swatch block (inference).</li></ul><p>Storybook:</p><ul><li>An MDX file with <code>&lt;Meta title=&quot;...&quot; /&gt;</code> and no <code>of</code> prop is a documentation-only page. Without <code>Meta</code> it still appears as a Docs entry by file location.</li><li>MDX embeds stories with <code>&lt;Canvas of=... /&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;Story of=... /&gt;</code>, renders an external <code>.md</code> with the <code>Markdown</code> block, and takes GitHub-style tables through <code>remark-gfm</code>.</li><li>Doc blocks include <code>ColorPalette</code>, <code>ColorItem</code>, <code>Typeset</code>, <code>IconGallery</code>, <code>Canvas</code>, <code>Story</code> and <code>Source</code>. These cover the swatches and type specimens directly.</li><li>Raw HTML and iframes are not covered on the MDX page, but MDX allows arbitrary JSX, so an <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> element should work (inference, not tested here). The MDX page&#39;s wording speaks of React components. The Storybook Vue docs were not checked for MDX specifics, so confirm in a spike that <code>&lt;Canvas&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;Story&gt;</code> render a Vue story in this repo.</li></ul><p>Android and iOS descriptions are plain prose, so either tool handles them as Markdown. Neither can show a Compose or SwiftUI component live.</p><h2 id="_3-interaction-tests-accessibility-ci" tabindex="-1">3. Interaction tests, accessibility, CI <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-interaction-tests-accessibility-ci" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Interaction tests, accessibility, CI&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Histoire</th><th>Storybook (already in the repo)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interaction tests</td><td><code>onTest</code> inside a story with <code>describe</code>, <code>it</code>, <code>expect</code> from Vitest, run with <code>histoire test</code>. Needs <code>vitest</code> and <code>@vitest/browser-playwright</code> on the same version, plus Playwright. No snapshot assertions, no fake timers</td><td><code>play</code> functions run by <code>@storybook/addon-vitest</code>: every story becomes a Vitest test in browser mode, and a play function&#39;s assertions are checked</td></tr><tr><td>Accessibility</td><td><code>@histoire/plugin-a11y</code> on <code>main</code>: axe-core 4.14.0, an Accessibility inspector tab, <code>test: true</code> adds an axe test per variant, and <code>assertA11y(canvas)</code> checks interaction states</td><td><code>@storybook/addon-a11y</code> on axe-core. <code>parameters.a11y.test</code> is <code>&#39;error&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;todo&#39;</code> or <code>&#39;off&#39;</code>, and <code>&#39;error&#39;</code> fails CI through the Vitest addon</td></tr><tr><td>Visual tests</td><td>Built in: a <code>visual</code> config block, <code>histoire visual</code> CLI with exit codes, baselines committed in <code>.histoire-visual/</code>, per-OS baselines</td><td>Chromatic (a cloud service), not used here</td></tr><tr><td>In CI</td><td><code>histoire test</code> and <code>histoire visual</code></td><td><code>vitest run</code> (the repo&#39;s <code>npm test -w storybook</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Published?</td><td>The plugin is not on npm (the registry returns &quot;Not found&quot; for <code>@histoire/plugin-a11y</code>), and the published <code>histoire@1.0.0-beta.1</code> declares no Vitest peers. So these features are <code>main</code> only</td><td>Published and in use</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The Histoire testing docs say the closest equivalent to &quot;light and dark a11y runs&quot; is not described; ADR 0028&#39;s &quot;in light and dark&quot; check would need a custom setup in either tool, and today it is only partly covered by the toolbar switch in Storybook (not verified whether both themes are tested in CI).</p><h2 id="_4-static-build-sub-path-light-and-dark" tabindex="-1">4. Static build, sub-path, light and dark <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-static-build-sub-path-light-and-dark" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. Static build, sub-path, light and dark&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Histoire:</p><ul><li><code>histoire build</code> is a static build, with output in <code>.histoire/dist</code> by default. A Node deployment target (<code>--target node</code>) is also new in beta.2+.</li><li>The base path is set with <code>vite: { base: &#39;/stories/&#39; }</code>. Embed URLs are built from it, and the whole built output must be deployed together (embedding guide).</li><li>The router has a <code>&#39;hash&#39;</code> mode, intended for static hosts without history fallback.</li><li>The toolbar has a light/dark button. It adds the class <code>dark</code> (config <code>theme.darkClass</code>) to the story preview, with <code>defaultColorScheme</code> of <code>&#39;light&#39;</code>, <code>&#39;dark&#39;</code> or <code>&#39;auto&#39;</code>, <code>hideColorSchemeSwitch</code> and <code>storeColorScheme</code>. This repo switches on <code>data-theme</code> on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> (<code>tokens.css</code>), so a setup file would have to translate (inference).</li></ul><p>Storybook:</p><ul><li><code>storybook build</code> gives a static app that &quot;any web server&quot; can serve. The publishing page does not discuss sub-paths. In this repo it is already built and copied to <code>site/public/storybook/</code> and shown at <code>/storybook/</code> (ADR 0028, <code>site/copy-docs.mjs</code>), so sub-path hosting is proven here, including with the docs site&#39;s <code>SITE_BASE</code>. Whether <code>SITE_BASE</code> is non-root in production, I did not check.</li><li>The theme switch is a toolbar global plus a decorator, and <code>web/storybook/.storybook/preview.ts</code> already sets <code>data-theme</code>. The Storybook toolbar docs say nothing about the docs pages&#39; theme, so MDX pages follow whatever the decorator and preview CSS do (not verified).</li></ul><h2 id="_5-reaching-it-from-the-docs-site-s-top-menu" tabindex="-1">5. Reaching it from the docs site&#39;s top menu <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-reaching-it-from-the-docs-site-s-top-menu" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. Reaching it from the docs site&#39;s top menu&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Both tools publish a self-contained static app, so both are reached the same way.</p><ul><li>The top menu is <code>themeConfig.nav</code> in <code>site/.vitepress/config.mts</code>, now five items, one of them &quot;Design system&quot; pointing at <code>/docs/design-system</code>.</li><li>VitePress nav <code>link</code> takes a full URL, or a path without <code>.md</code> that must start with <code>/</code>. It sets <code>target</code> and <code>rel</code> itself for external links and lets you override them. The nav page does not say how to link to a static folder under <code>public</code>, so test <code>link: &#39;/storybook/&#39;</code> (and <code>target: &#39;_self&#39;</code>) on a PR preview, with <code>/storybook/index.html</code> as the fallback (unverified).</li><li>To make it the one home: add the MDX pages, replace the &quot;Design system&quot; nav item with a link to the built Storybook, drop the section&#39;s sidebar key, and keep <code>StoryFrame</code> embeds only where another page needs one.</li><li>The cost is that readers leave the VitePress theme, search and sidebar when they click that menu item. The owner&#39;s wish for &quot;one place&quot; accepts this. A Storybook-side link back to the docs site (a <code>brandTitle</code> or a sidebar link) would soften it, but I did not check how Storybook 10 sets that.</li><li>For Histoire the same plan applies, with <code>base</code> set to the sub-path and the output copied to <code>site/public/</code>.</li></ul><h2 id="_6-verdict-for-this-repo" tabindex="-1">6. Verdict for this repo <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-verdict-for-this-repo" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. Verdict for this repo&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Stay on Storybook.</p><ul><li>It is released and moving every week or two. Histoire&#39;s useful new parts (Vite 8, <code>histoire test</code>, the a11y plugin) are not published.</li><li>The repo already has Storybook&#39;s tests, axe check and theme switch working in CI, and ADR 0028 already weighed Histoire and chose against it for the same reason. Nothing found here changes that, though the ADR&#39;s line &quot;updated less often&quot; is now confirmed by the npm record and its &quot;thinner testing&quot; is out of date on <code>main</code> (but not in a release).</li><li>Histoire&#39;s standalone Markdown pages are a good match for prose, but live samples, swatches and iframes would all be Vue stories, which Storybook&#39;s MDX and doc blocks do more directly.</li><li>Revisit Histoire only if a stable 1.0 ships with Vite 8, <code>histoire test</code> and the a11y plugin, and Storybook becomes a burden.</li></ul><p>Suggested next step if the owner agrees: a spike that adds three MDX pages to <code>web/storybook</code> (a written-rules page from <code>docs/design-system.md</code>, a tokens page with <code>ColorPalette</code>, and a mockups page with iframes), then checks the sub-path and the nav link on a PR preview. That would also answer the points marked unverified above.</p><h2 id="not-verified" tabindex="-1">Not verified <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-verified" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Not verified&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Histoire&#39;s commit and issue activity (GitHub API access refused). The cadence is from npm and the changelog only.</li><li>Whether Histoire&#39;s <code>html: true</code> Markdown renders an <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> in the workbench.</li><li>Whether MDX <code>&lt;Canvas&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;Story&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> work with <code>@storybook/vue3-vite</code> here.</li><li>How a VitePress nav link to a folder in <code>public</code> behaves.</li><li>Whether Histoire&#39;s Tailwind 4 support is official (no statement found).</li></ul><h2 id="sources" tabindex="-1">Sources <a class="header-anchor" href="#sources" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sources&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Histoire npm record (versions and dates, peer ranges): <a href="https://registry.npmjs.org/histoire," target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://registry.npmjs.org/histoire,</a> <a href="https://registry.npmjs.org/histoire/1.0.0-beta.1," target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://registry.npmjs.org/histoire/1.0.0-beta.1,</a> <a href="https://registry.npmjs.org/@histoire/plugin-vue/1.0.0-beta.1," target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://registry.npmjs.org/@histoire/plugin-vue/1.0.0-beta.1,</a> <code>https://registry.npmjs.org/@histoire/plugin-a11y</code> (a 404, so not published)</li><li>Histoire changelog: <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/CHANGELOG.md</a></li><li>Histoire releases: <a href="https://github.com/histoire-dev/histoire/releases" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://github.com/histoire-dev/histoire/releases</a></li><li>Histoire <code>main</code> package manifest and engines: <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/package.json," target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/package.json,</a> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/packages/histoire/package.json" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/packages/histoire/package.json</a></li><li>Histoire getting started: <a href="https://histoire.dev/guide/getting-started.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://histoire.dev/guide/getting-started.html</a> (source: <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/docs/guide/getting-started.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/docs/guide/getting-started.md</a>)</li><li>Histoire documentation and standalone pages: <a href="https://histoire.dev/guide/vue3/docs" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://histoire.dev/guide/vue3/docs</a> (source: <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/docs/guide/vue3/docs.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/docs/guide/vue3/docs.md</a>)</li><li>Histoire Markdown renderer: <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/packages/histoire/src/node/markdown/renderer.ts" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/packages/histoire/src/node/markdown/renderer.ts</a></li><li>Histoire testing: <a href="https://histoire.dev/guide/testing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://histoire.dev/guide/testing</a></li><li>Histoire visual testing: <a href="https://histoire.dev/guide/visual-testing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://histoire.dev/guide/visual-testing</a></li><li>Histoire accessibility plugin: <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/docs/guide/plugins/a11y.md," target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/docs/guide/plugins/a11y.md,</a> official plugin list <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/docs/guide/plugins/official.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/docs/guide/plugins/official.md</a></li><li>Histoire embedding and base path: <a href="https://histoire.dev/guide/embedding" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://histoire.dev/guide/embedding</a></li><li>Histoire configuration: <a href="https://histoire.dev/guide/config," target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://histoire.dev/guide/config,</a> <a href="https://histoire.dev/reference/config" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://histoire.dev/reference/config</a> (source: <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/docs/reference/config.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/docs/reference/config.md</a>)</li><li>Histoire Node deployment: <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/docs/guide/deploy-node.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://raw.githubusercontent.com/histoire-dev/histoire/main/docs/guide/deploy-node.md</a></li><li>Storybook npm record: <a href="https://registry.npmjs.org/storybook," target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://registry.npmjs.org/storybook,</a> <a href="https://registry.npmjs.org/@storybook/vue3-vite" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://registry.npmjs.org/@storybook/vue3-vite</a></li><li>Storybook MDX: <a href="https://storybook.js.org/docs/writing-docs/mdx" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://storybook.js.org/docs/writing-docs/mdx</a></li><li>Storybook doc blocks: <a href="https://storybook.js.org/docs/writing-docs/doc-blocks" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://storybook.js.org/docs/writing-docs/doc-blocks</a></li><li>Storybook Vitest addon: <a href="https://storybook.js.org/docs/writing-tests/integrations/vitest-addon" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://storybook.js.org/docs/writing-tests/integrations/vitest-addon</a></li><li>Storybook accessibility testing: <a href="https://storybook.js.org/docs/writing-tests/accessibility-testing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://storybook.js.org/docs/writing-tests/accessibility-testing</a></li><li>Storybook publishing: <a href="https://storybook.js.org/docs/sharing/publish-storybook" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://storybook.js.org/docs/sharing/publish-storybook</a></li><li>Storybook toolbars and globals: <a href="https://storybook.js.org/docs/essentials/toolbars-and-globals" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://storybook.js.org/docs/essentials/toolbars-and-globals</a></li><li>VitePress nav: <a href="https://vitepress.dev/reference/default-theme-nav" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://vitepress.dev/reference/default-theme-nav</a></li><li>In this repo: <code>docs/adr/0023-the-websites-share-one-vue-and-daisyui-design-system.md</code>, <code>docs/adr/0028-storybook-is-the-component-workshop-of-the-design-system.md</code>, <code>web/storybook/package.json</code>, <code>web/storybook/.storybook/main.ts</code>, <code>web/storybook/.storybook/preview.ts</code>, <code>site/.vitepress/config.mts</code>, <code>site/copy-docs.mjs</code></li></ul>',36)])])}const b=t(r,[["render",a]]);export{p as __pageData,b as default};
